That's

> On Mar 1, 2016, at 7:40 AM, Chris <wel...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Hi Bob and others,
>
> I got a three component mixture working with
>
>   for (n in 1:N) {
>     increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(log(theta) +
>       normal_log(y[n], X[n,]*betas1, sigma[1]) +
>       normal_log(y[n], X[n,]*betas2, sigma[2]) +
>       normal_log(y[n], X[n,]*betas3, sigma[3])));
>   }
>

I'm afraid that's not a 3-component mixture.  Assuming theta is
a 3-simplex, the mixture would look as follows (assuming betas is made
into a vector):

  for (n in 1:N) {

    vector[3] lp;
    lp <- log(theta);
    for (k in 1:3)
      lp[k] <- normal_log(y[n], X[n] * betas[k], sigma[k]);
    increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(lp));
  }

There's a discussion of why the algebra is this way
in the manual chapter on mixtures.

> Can you explain to me please how I would proceed to define a mixture where the number of mixture components K
> is also estimated?

If you're thinking of Dirichlet processes, we don't really do
that in Stan.  You could technically make a mixture of mixtures
and evaluate this, but it's usually not worth the effort.

> And how do I calculate the log likelihood for this model in the generated quantities part?

Just follow the likelihood definition in the model block,
only use foo_log(y, ...) rather than y ~ foo(...).

> the log likelihood of a three component mixture with distributions f and mixing proportion p for n observations is log(prod_n(p1*f1 + p2*f2 + p3*f3)).

I don't know what prod_n is.

- Bob

