Thanks, Jonah --- that should definitely be

  lp[k] <- lp[k] + normal_log(...);

I probably messed up the original suggestion.

You don't want to repeat the code, though.  Break
the likelihood out into a function and just call it
in both the model block and generated quantities block.

Or you could define the likelihood as a transformed
parameter and then just use increment_log_prob(...) on
it in the model block.

- Bob

> On Mar 2, 2016, at 3:22 PM, Jonah Gabry <jga...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> On Wednesday, March 2, 2016 at 2:52:31 PM UTC-5, Chris wrote:
>
> thanks for making that more clear. Could you please advise how I turn my likelihood into a log-likelihood in the generated quantities section.
> I'm a little confused because I have so many "logs" in my likelihood already.
>
> This is the model likelihood:
>
> >   for (n in 1:N) {
> >     vector[3] lp;
> >     lp <- log(theta);
> >     for (k in 1:3)
> >       lp[k] <- normal_log(y[n], X[n] * betas[k], sigma[k]);
> >     increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(lp));
> >   }
>
>
> I might be missing something but I think you want to add to lp[k] instead of redefining it. Right now you set lp equal to log(theta) but then set it equal to something else when you set lp[k] to the result of normal_log(...). So I think you want lp[k] <- lp[k] + normal_log(...).
>
> In generated quantities you should be able to do exactly the same thing you do in the model block but instead of incrementing the log probability you just want to save its value.
>
> Jonah
