On Sunday, March 6, 2016 at 12:01:37 PM UTC-5, Stéphane Laurent wrote:
> Hello,
>
> This is my first attempt to fit a multivariate normal distribution with (r)stan using a LKJ prior. My code is given below. I set a zero mean so that the covariance matrix is the only unknown parameter.
>
> I get some numerical problems :
>
> > validate transformed params: Sigma is not positive definite
> > validate transformed params: Sigma is not symmetric. Sigma[1,2] = -nan, but Sigma[2,1] = -nan
> > Exception thrown at line 23: lkj_corr_log: y is not positive definite
> > validate transformed params: Sigma is not symmetric. Sigma[1,2] = inf, but Sigma[2,1] = inf
>
> Is it important ? Is there a way to avoid these problems ?
> This apart, the results look quite good.

A couple numerical failures is not that bad and fixing them won't change your results. But you can
Declare Sigma as a matrix rather than a cov_matrix, in which case it will not check that it is symmetric and positive definite. This should only be done when the expression for Sigma is positive definite by construction, which it is in your case.
Better, use a Cholesky factor of Sigma instead. In that case you would put
parameters {
  cholesky_factor_corr[J] L;
  vector<lower=0>[J] sigma;
}
model {
  y ~ multi_normal_cholesky(zero, diag_pre_multiply(sigma, L));
  sigma ~ gamma(0.1, 0.1);
  L ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(nu);
}
generated quantities {
  matrix[J,J] Omega;
  matrix[J,J] Sigma;
  Omega <- multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L);
  Sigma <- quad_form_diag(sigma, Omega);
}

It is always much better from a numerical / speed perspective to utilize Cholesky factors of symmetric positive definite matrices rather than the symmetric positive definite matrices themselves.

Ben

