On Sunday, March 6, 2016 at 1:53:44 PM UTC-5, Stéphane Laurent wrote:
> Thank you ! I will try that.
> As a side question, is there a difference between generated quantities and transformed parameters ?

Yes, there is a large difference. The objects declared in the transformed parameters block are intended to be used in the likelihood of the data and hence are evaluated every time the log-posterior is evaluated, which can be thousands of times per iteration, and the autodiff mechanism has to be used in order to evaluate the derivative of the log-posterior with respect to each of the parameters declared in the parameters block. The objects declared in the generated quantities block are not intended to be used in the likelihood of the data, and hence are only evaluated once per iteration and do not require autodiff. In addition, objects declared in the transformed parameters block can be checked for validity (although this is really only good for catching programming mistakes) whereas the objects declared in the generated quantities block (and model block) are not validated, which saves time.

Ben

