Yes, normal(0, 1e5) is not realistic. As Andrew suggested, I have rerun my model with normal(0,10) and normal(0,5). Results are now consistent with those from the original publication.

> > I get a non-zero estimate for home. That also eliminates my previous question about flat priors. All good!
> >
> > Stan-derived estimates for mu_att and mu_def are not informative,
>
> If the model's right and Stan converges, these will be *the* Bayesian posterior
> mean estimates give or take MCMC std error.

Ah I noticed a mistake in my code, where I had accidentally set the prior for att_free as

att_free[i] ~ normal(0, sig_att);

instead of
att_free[i] ~ normal(mu_att, sig_att);
Similar for def_free.
This lead to mu_att being sampled freely from the normal(0, 1e5) distribution.

>
> > for example mean(mu_att) +- sd(mu_att) = 696 +- 99762.
> > I was expecting stan to drive mu_att and mu_def to zero due to shrinkage. Could somebody point out why this is not the case?
>
> Well, that looks pretty close to zero on its scale if you didn't
> skip any decimal points.  Posterior densities are only as
> tight as the data allows.

See my previous comment/correction.

>
> > Also, thanks Bob for tidying up my stan model code; I was aware of certain vectorisations; others are new to me ("array slicing and indexing"). I usually leave for-loops in my stan models to help with (initial) readability and debugging.
>
> It's less error prone to vectorize once you get used to it.
> We have that issue in deciding how to present models the
> first time to users.
>
> > One more question about the vectorisation and code optimisation: Let's say I would like to store estimates for parameters theta_home and theta_away; would it be faster to do what you did (i.e. use the poisson_log) and then inverse-transform the log_theta's back to the linear scale in a separate generated quantities block?
>
> Yes.  Use the log-scale distributions for sampling --- they're
> more arithmetically stable and faster.

Great. Thanks for clearing that up.

>
> You can regenerate the values in generated quantities,
> which is fast because there are no gradients involved.
>
> - Bob
>
> > Or use poisson and theta_home, theta_away parameters in the transformed parameters block like in my original example?
> >
> > Thanks,
> > Maurits
> >
> >
> >
> >  Tuesday, 8 March 2016 07:49:08 UTC+11, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > Stan uses the scale (std dev) parameterization of
> > normal, like R, so this is probably not what you
> > meant:
> >
> >   home ~ normal(0, 0.0001);
> >
> > Those diffuse gamma priors can also be problematic; we
> > usually recommend half-Cauchy or even half-normal.
> >
> > Also, you can vectorize to make this all much much
> > faster and the code much simpler. This:
> >
> >  for (i in 1:ngames) {
> >    theta_home[i] <- exp(home + att[team_home[i]] + def[team_away[i]]);
> >    theta_away[i] <- exp(att[team_away[i]] + def[team_home[i]]);
> >  }
> >
> >  for (i in 1:ngames) {
> >    score_home[i] ~ poisson(theta_home[i]);
> >    score_away[i] ~ poisson(theta_away[i]);
> >  }
> >
> > can be replaced with:
> >
> >  for (i in 1:ngames) {
> >    log_theta_home[i] <- home + att[team_home[i]] + def[team_away[i]];
> >    log_theta_away[i] <- att[team_away[i]] + def[team_home[i]];
> >  }
> >
> >   score_home ~ poisson_log(log_theta_home);
> >   score_away ~ poisson_log(log_theta_away);
> >
> > The poisson_log function takes its parameter on the log scale.
> >
> > With our new array slicing and indexing, both of these can
> > be replaced with the two-liner:
> >
> >   score_home ~ poisson_log(home + att[team_home] + def[team_away]);
> >   score_away ~ poisson_log(att[team_away] + def[team_home]);
> >
> > This isn't even in the manual yet --- I need to update it
> > ASAP with examples!
> >
> > - Bob
> >
> > > On Mar 7, 2016, at 3:37 PM, Maurits Evers <maurit...@gmail.com> wrote:
