Date: Sat, 12 Mar 2016 02:05:34 +0000
Subject: A fast way to calculate the bivariate poisson in STAN
Message-ID: <SUcp-ktkXn4.-MiTkmSwBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/SUcp-ktkXn4/m/-MiTkmSwBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

For math details  and specification look in:

http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/108705/deriving-the-bivariate-poisson-distribution <http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/108705/deriving-the-bivariate-poisson-distribution>

For speed reasons miny is used as a parameter: miny := min(r,s)
mu1, mu2, mu3 must be provided on log scale.

The return value is the probability mass function of the bivariate poisson on the log scale.

   real bipois2(int r,int s,real mu1,real mu2,real mu3,int miny);

   real bipois2(int r,int s,real mu1,real mu2,real mu3, int miny) {
     real ss;
     real log_s;
     real mus;

     ss <- poisson_log_log(r,mu1) + poisson_log_log(s,mu2) - exp(mu3);
     if(miny > 0) {
       mus <- -mu1-mu2+mu3;
       log_s <- ss;

       for(k in 1:miny) {
            log_s <- log_s + log(r - k + 1) + mus
                           + log(s - k + 1)
                           - log(k);
            ss <- log_sum_exp(ss, log_s);
       }
     }
     return(ss);
   }

Andre

